WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
Bash scripts often run outside an interactive terminal.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:14.680
They run in CI/CD jobs, cron schedules, systemd services, containers, remote administration sessions, deployment agents, and configuration-management workflows.

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.460
Each environment has its own identity and permission model.

4
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:26.160
A script that looks harmless in a local shell may have much broader reach when executed by a pipeline runner or a service account.

5
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.080
CI/CD jobs deserve particular care.

6
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:38.780
A job may receive repository write access, package publishing rights, cloud credentials, deployment tokens, environment variables, and artifact permissions.

7
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:46.160
Scope those permissions to the minimum job, branch, project, environment, or role that needs them.

8
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.180
A test job usually does not need production deployment credentials.

9
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:55.560
A pull request job should not automatically receive the same authority as a trusted release job.

10
00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.560
Scheduled jobs and services should have explicit execution identities.

11
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:06.500
A cron entry that runs as a powerful local user can quietly become an administrative interface.

12
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:13.880
A systemd service can often be assigned a dedicated user, narrower filesystem access, and clearer service boundaries.

13
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:25.740
Containers also need review: user identity, mounted directories, network access, injected secrets, and runtime privileges all affect what the script can do from inside the container.

14
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:33.540
Remote jobs inherit boundaries from SSH, sudo, deployment agents, and the target host.

15
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.720
Avoid one identity that works everywhere simply because it is convenient.

16
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:42.060
Separate development, staging, and production where practical.

17
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.360
Review which hosts, networks, secrets, and files the automation can reach.

18
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:59.060
Lifecycle matters too: when a job is retired, remove stale access, tokens, schedules, and remote permissions so yesterday's automation does not remain tomorrow's hidden privilege.
